      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2017 Wiley-VCH Verlag GmbH & Co. KGaA, Weinheim.",
        "AbstractText": "The surface of ice has been reported to be disordered at temperatures well below the bulk melting point. However, the precise nature of this disorder has been a topic of intense debate. Herein, we study the molecular properties of the surface of ice as a function of temperatures using heterodyne-detected sum-frequency generation spectroscopy. We observe that, down to 245\u2005K, the spectral response of the surface of ice contains a component that is indistinguishable from supercooled liquid water."
      },
